# Error Handling — Fail Loudly

Agents are RL-optimized to produce code that runs, so they over-protect
locally: broad catches, silent fallbacks to defaults. That is the opposite of
good design — a service silently running with wrong config is worse than a
service that refuses to start.

## Rules

- **Failures propagate up.** Never catch-and-continue unless the catch site
  can genuinely handle the error (retry, compensate, surface to the user).
- **No silent defaults on config/startup paths.** If a config file, env var,
  or secret fails to load, crash with a clear message — do not fall back to
  defaults that let the service hobble along.
- **No bare catch-alls.** Every catch block must do at least one of: rethrow
  (enriched), route to the structured logger with context, or execute a real
  recovery path. Empty blocks are linted (`suspicious/noEmptyBlockStatements`,
  currently `warn` — 66 pre-existing violations; promote to `error` after the
  cleanup sweep). Never add new ones.
- **Catch narrowly.** Prefer catching at the boundary that can act (handler,
  job runner, capability invoker) over wrapping every call site.
- Fallback behavior that masks a failure (default config, empty list on query
  error, retry-forever) requires an explicit comment justifying why masking is
  correct there.

```ts
// ❌ Bad — service hobbles along with wrong config
let config: Config;
try {
  config = loadConfig(path);
} catch {
  config = DEFAULT_CONFIG;
}

// ✅ Good — refuses to start, failure is visible immediately
const config = loadConfig(path); // throws with a clear message
```
